    a new way to fail? What is the ACTUAL residual risk — is it binding, or is it the
    FIDELITY of the diff as a rendering of the commit? Enumerate every way `git diff base..tip`
    can under-represent what a merge will actually bring in: binary files, submodules,
    symlinks (mode 120000), file mode/exec-bit changes, .gitattributes diff drivers /
    textconv / `-diff`, `.git/config` written from inside a linked worktree, renames with
    -M, CRLF/`core.autocrlf`, empty-tree/no-common-history, merge commits on the branch,
    a branch that itself merges main, `git diff` two-dot vs three-dot semantics.

Q2. MERGE-BASE DRIFT. If main advances between review and merge, `git merge-base main tip`
    changes, so recomputing the same command at merge time yields a DIFFERENT diff than the
    panel reviewed — even though tip never moved. Does this make the proposed equality check
    fail-closed-but-useless (every merge refused whenever main moves)? What should be
    recorded and re-verified instead?

Q3. MODE SELECTION. The gatekeeper must know whether a job is "single-file text" or
    "diff-reviewed" to pick the right rule. Where must that fact live so it cannot be flipped
